@startuml

title A firing that opens a run: where the schedule's part ends

participant "the scheduler" as scheduler
database "the firing record" as record
participant "the run's owner\n(ores.reporting, for a report)" as owner
database "the owner's rows\n(a report instance)" as domain
participant "the workflow engine\n(process manager)" as engine
database "the run state" as run
participant "the caller" as caller

scheduler -> record : record the firing as starting\n(the job, the point, the moment)
scheduler -> owner : publish the trigger\n(the subject the run's owner declares;\nthe firing's identity travels in it)
activate owner
owner -> domain : create the row the run acts on
owner -> engine : publish the start message\n(workflow.v1.start; the run's identity\nis named here; no reply subject)
owner --> scheduler : answer
deactivate owner

note right of scheduler
    The job neither waits for the run
    nor reads its state. The trigger is
    a request, and the reply says only
    that it was accepted.
end note

scheduler -> record : record the firing succeeded\n(the trigger was accepted;\nthe run is not read)

engine -> run : create the run\n(from the definition the start names)
engine -> engine : dispatch the declared steps,\none at a time
engine -> run : record each step's outcome
engine --> run : the run ends when the last\nstep completes

... the run continues ...

caller -> run : follow the run through\nthe progress read

note over engine, run
    The contents of the run, and how it ends,
    belong to the workflow cluster. The firing
    records only that the trigger was accepted.
end note

@enduml
